  "body": "Currently [HTML](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/rendering.html#the-input-element-as-a-text-entry-widget:event-select) says:\r\n\r\n>If these text controls provide a text selection, then, ___when the user changes the current selection___, the user agent is expected to queue an element task on the user interaction task source given the input element to fire an event named select at the element, with the bubbles attribute initialized to true.\r\n\r\nBut this is not entirely true on browsers. Some examples on Gecko and Blink:\r\n\r\n* Collapsing selection via user input e.g. mouse and keyboard does not fire a select event\r\n* Moving the cursor does not fire a select event\r\n* Shift+Mouse Click fires a select event when the selection is not collapsed, even if the selection is not changed\r\n\r\nAnd this is inconsistent with the selection setter APIs e.g. `setSelectionRange()` as they do fire select events when and only when any selection change happens.\r\n\r\nSince UI Events defers the behavior definition to host languages, this needs to be specified better in HTML.\r\n\r\ncc @masayuki-nakano",
